  "answer": "(a) RR3 timeline (quantum 3, switch cost 1, boundary=before, during_switch=tail, early_finish_free):\n  0-3   run A (3 units; A has 3 left)\n  3-4   switch A -> B (cost 1)\n  4-7   run B (3 units; B has 1 left; C arrived at 2, already queued)\n  7-8   switch B -> C (cost 1)\n  8-10  run C (2 units; C finishes before its slice ends) -> the next switch is free\n  10-13 run A (3 units; A finishes exactly at slice end)\n  13-14 switch A -> B (cost 1)\n  14-15 run B (1 unit; B finishes)\nQueue evolution: t=0 [A]; t=1 B arrives -> [B]; t=2 C arrives -> [B,C]; at t=3 slice end, A (arrival-order waiting processes keep their position, and boundary=before means any arrival at exactly t=3 would go before A) is requeued -> [B,C,A]; after the active process B is selected, the 3-4 switch runs; t=7 slice end requeues B -> [C,A,B]; C selected; t=10 C finishes early, next switch free, A resumes; t=13 A finishes exactly at slice end, requeue [B]; t=13-14 switch to B; t=14-15 B's last unit.\nPer-job RR3 metrics: A first run 0, response 0, completion 13, turnaround 13; B first run 4, response 3, completion 15, turnaround 14; C first run 8, response 6, completion 10, turnaround 8.\n\n(b) RR3 averages: average response time = (0+3+6)/3 = 3.0; average turnaround time = (13+14+8)/3 = 35/3 ≈ 11.67. Total switching time = 4 charged switches x 1 = 3 time units (the A->B at 3, the B->C at 7, and the A->B at 13; the switch after C's early finish at t=10 is free). CPU service = 6+4+2 = 12, so makespan = 12 + 3 = 15, matching completion of B at 15.\n\n(c) STCF timeline (zero switch cost, preempt on strictly smaller remaining time, ties keep the running job):\n  0-1   run A (A has 5 left)\n  1-2   run B (at t=1 B arrives with 4 < A's 5, preempt)\n  2-4   run C (at t=2 C arrives with 2 < B's 3, preempt; C finishes)\n  4-7   run B (B has 3 left; A has 5, so B continues to completion)\n  7-12  run A (final 5 units)\nCompletions: C at 4, B at 7, A at 12. Turnarounds: A 12-0 = 12, B 7-1 = 6, C 4-2 = 2; average = (12+6+2)/3 = 20/3 ≈ 6.67. STCF average response time = (0+0+0)/3 = 0 and it is also faster overall (makespan 12 vs 15).\nWhy the RR3 timeline is this way: A runs first because at t=0 only A has arrived. C finishes at time 10 because it needs only 2 units and reaches the CPU at t=8, the first moment after both A's and B's first slices (0-3 and 4-7) plus switches; being shortest, it completes within its 3-unit quantum, so the switch afterwards is free. B runs only 1 unit at t=14 because B needs 4 units total and already received 3 in its slice at 4-7; only 1 unit of its burst remains, and it completes at t=15 after the paid A->B switch at 13-14.\n\n(d) Decision: only STCF fits the budget. RR3 spends 3 time units switching, exactly at the stated limit, so it is only acceptable if 3 is read as an inclusive (at-most-3) cap; it cannot absorb any extra context switch, and any additional overhead (e.g., a longer quantum or an extra job) would break the budget. STCF has zero switching cost by assumption, so it uses 0 of the 3-unit budget and is comfortably compliant.\nObjective trade-off: RR3's merit is early interleaved access (A first runs at 0, B at 4, C at 8, i.e., all three jobs get on the CPU before any turnaround is settled) and it is fairer in the short run; STCF instead gives the best overall completion behavior here, with an average turnaround of 6.67 vs 11.67 for RR3 and a smaller makespan (12 vs 15). The metric sacrificed by choosing STCF is responsiveness/fairness: STCF's *average* response is actually 0 here (each job first runs at its arrival, because arrivals preempt immediately), but its worst-case individual wait and its starvation risk are exposed - a long job like A is preempted repeatedly and finishes last, so a job with a long burst can be delayed arbitrarily by a stream of short arrivals, whereas RR3 bounds each job's wait by roughly one quantum plus switch time.\nWorkload fact needed before trusting the recommendation: whether the workload is really a set of independent, known-length single-burst jobs with no I/O. STCF's advantage depends on knowing each job's total CPU service in advance (the question assumes this) and on jobs not doing I/O or repeated bursts; in a real workload with unknown or interactive/mixed bursts, or with longer jobs arriving after short ones, STCF's starvation and cost of mispredicted lengths (and RR3's actual switch overhead on a real CPU) could reverse the comparison.",
